w years is these unconventional  ways of doing monetary policy of trying  to support growth are not as effective  as as the normal tools so the Fed is now  done more than two trillion dollars  they're doing 85 billion dollars a month  right now in an additional easing trying  to buy more bonds and it hasn't you know  it's been enough to keep us away from  deflation we haven't had falling prices  like the Japanese have had for the last  20 years we haven't had you know a  double dip into recession but we also  haven't had the kind of robust growth  that everybody is trying to get so the  question is are they at the end of their  Road have they done everything they can  or or are we out of tools and I think  what we've seen out of the bernanke fed  this last few years  is a is a real capacity to to look  around and say okay what's working  what's not it's not working we're going  to dust off the playbook and try  something new and that's what they've  been doing since sep tember with  communications so they said in starting  in September and then December all right  we're not just going to buy all these  bonds we're also going to use  communications we're going to tell you  what we're going to do and it's this  we're going to try and keep pumping  money into the economy until we're on  track we're going to keep interest rates  very low until either unemployment drops  to six and a half percent or inflation  becomes a problem and get sub up two and  a half percent they're trying to use  communications and a sense that you know  this is the plan to instill confidence  in the economy and get growth on track  so far there's some evidence the  economy's doing a little better this  year but it's it's pretty modest now the  question is what does all this activism  that all the global central banks have  been doing for the last five years  what's the cost and what's what are the  risks this creates well you know the  truth is we have been an uncharted  territory and this true